Step-by-Step: Add a Second Cluster to Your Existing File

Generate token for the new cluster (repeat for each cluster)

Bash# Example for Cluster-2
TOKEN2=$(kubectl --kubeconfig /path/to/other-cluster-kubeconfig create token app-team-sa -n app-ns --duration=8760h)

Append the new cluster to your existing app-team-kubeconfig.yaml

Run this command to add a new cluster (replace values accordingly):
Bashcat >> /opt/mirantis/app-team-kubeconfig.yaml << EOF

# ==================== Second Cluster ====================
clusters:
- cluster:
    certificate-authority-data: $(kubectl --kubeconfig /opt/mirantis/my-onprem-cluster-kubeconfig1.yaml config view --raw --minify -o jsonpath='{.clusters[0].cluster.certificate-authority-data}')
    server: https://10.109.161.55:6443        # Change to new cluster VIP or IP
  name: my-onprem-cluster-2

contexts:
- context:
    cluster: my-onprem-cluster-2
    namespace: app-ns
    user: app-team-sa-2
  name: app-team-context-2

users:
- name: app-team-sa-2
  user:
    token: $TOKEN2
EOF
Full Clean Example of Multi-Cluster Kubeconfig
Here’s what a good multi-cluster app-team-kubeconfig.yaml looks like:
YAMLapiVersion: v1
kind: Config
current-context: app-team-context-1     # Change this to switch default cluster

clusters:
- cluster:
    certificate-authority-data: PASTE_CA_DATA_CLUSTER1_HERE
    server: https://10.109.161.55:6443
  name: my-onprem-cluster-1

- cluster:
    certificate-authority-data: PASTE_CA_DATA_CLUSTER2_HERE
    server: https://NEW-VIP-OR-IP:6443
  name: my-onprem-cluster-2

contexts:
- context:
    cluster: my-onprem-cluster-1
    namespace: app-ns
    user: app-team-sa-1
  name: app-team-context-1

- context:
    cluster: my-onprem-cluster-2
    namespace: app-ns
    user: app-team-sa-2
  name: app-team-context-2

users:
- name: app-team-sa-1
  user:
    token: TOKEN_FOR_CLUSTER1_HERE

- name: app-team-sa-2
  user:
    token: TOKEN_FOR_CLUSTER2_HERE
Daily Usage Commands
Bashexport KUBECONFIG=/opt/mirantis/app-team-kubeconfig.yaml

# List all available contexts
kubectl config get-contexts

# Switch between clusters
kubectl config use-context app-team-context-1
kubectl config use-context app-team-context-2

# Run command on specific context without switching
kubectl --context app-team-context-2 get pods -n app-ns
Pro Tips for Maintenance

Keep the file in a secure location (~/.kube/app-team-kubeconfig.yaml is common).
Use meaningful context names: prod-cluster, staging-cluster, edge-store961.
Backup the file regularly.
To update a token later: just edit the file and replace the token value.
You can add as many clusters as you want.

